Date: Thu, 31 Dec 1992 15:59:55 -0800
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: Re: "floptical" disks

>> ...I got an ad in the mail about 2 weeks ago from a
>> company selling what they call "floptical" drives for the Mac.  They
>> take disks that look like 3 1/2" floppies, but hold 20meg each.  The
>> drive was, I believe, $400.  Does anyone know anything about these?
>...
>>One version: the optical technology is used only for the extremely precise
>alignment of the head, which then uses conventional magnetic methods for
>recording the data.  

"Floptical" is the trademark for one standard that works as described 
above.  Floptical drives also read and write standard high-density Mac and
DOS 3.5" floppies, but not Mac 800K disks.

I got mine, a "Viper Drive," for $450.  Either something's buggy or it was 
the slow death of the separate power supply (one of those little bricks you 
plug into the wall), but mine would work fine for a while and then hang the 
machine.  I bought it because I love the technology, knowing I might 
have these kinds of problems.  ("Never buy version 1.x or version x.0 of 
anything!")  I haven't sent in the brick for replacement yet, so my jury's
still out on the "Viper" brand.  The disks are transparent brown with a 
slight rainbow effect on one side.

For a while there was a problem getting the media (the actual disks), but
now 3M is making them, and you should be able to get them for about $17
a piece.  I called 3M and asked for distributors in my area.

There is another, competing standard that uses the same technology but a 
slightly different format, and those drives can't deal with standard 
high-density disks.  I haven't heard much about this standard lately. 

>Second:  a lazer heats up (melts?) a small spot on the disk surface, then a
>conventional magnetic head magnetizes it to be a "1" or "0".  The advantage
>is that much smaller "spots" can be used, since the adjacent areas that are
>also subjected to the magnetic field do not change their polarity since
>they are not heated by the laser.

Here we're talking about magneto-optical disks.  There are, for instance,
128Meg 3.5" magneto-optical disks.  The laser only heats up the material,
(doesn't melt it) which temporarily increases its ability to be magnetized.
I think they read it by shining polarized light at it, which reflects 
differently off different magnetic fields.

