Date: Fri, 1 Jan 1993 17:24:43 -0800
From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: LING: Aryans, Hindus (and, oh yeah, Hitler)

I hesitate to assert my knowledge of India over that of someone named Sameer
Parekh, but I *did* study Sanskrit for a semester or two, and have a broad
layman's grasp of Indo-European linguistics, so here goes....
 
As most of you know, in the nineteenth century it was perceived that most of
the languages of Europe and India have a common origin.  Today we call the
language family "Indo-European", but it has also been called "Indo-Germanic"
(after its two most far-flung branches) and, loosely, "Aryan".
 
A major branch of the family is Indo-Iranian.  "Arya(n)" is the Indo-Iranian
languages' name for their own people.  It is related to the Greek "ar-ist-"
(best).  The Aryans, relatively fair-skinned Indic-speakers, conquered India
and lord it over the dark Dravidians.
 
The parallels between the ideal Aryans of India and the ideal Germans of
Hitler's dreams are obvious, and the kinship of Teutonic with Aryan
languages reinforced the analogy -- overextended, perhaps, as exciting new
paradigms often are, and I daresay Indo-European was still excitingly new in
Hitler's youth.  (Of course, the same language family includes the speech of
the Slavs and Mediterraneans whom Hitler would put in the Dravidian role.  I
have no idea whether that bothered him.)
 
The priestly class's name, BRAAHMANA, is related to that of BRAH-MAA or
BRAHMAN, the creator god who gave language (BRAH).  The priests, whose color
is white, came from the mouth of Brahmaa.
 
The warrior class, KSATRIYA, is next; they take their name from KSATRAM,
which I think means `authority'.  The warriors, whose color is red, came
from the arms of Brahmaa.
 
The merchant/farmer/artisan class, VAIS'YA, whose color is yellow, came from
the "thighs" (genitals) of Brahmaa.  Their name appears to me to come from a
verb meaning `enter, occupy, settle'.
 
The servile class, S'UDRA, whose color is black, came from the feet of
Brahmaa.  As non-Aryans, they are excluded from at least some religious
practices.  (The Buddha is counted as an avatar of Visnu who came to mislead
S'udras who were performing sacrifices and asceticisms!)
 
You may notice that I have avoided the word "caste", which has another
narrower meaning.  Within each of the four orders (for which the Sanskrit
word is VARNA `color') are many castes, i.e. hereditary occupations.
 
I think, though I'm not sure, that the Untouchables are outside the
varna-structure.
 
