Date: Fri, 1 Jan 1993 17:25:39 -0800
From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: libel, coercion and polysemy

Robin, I'm missing something.  Mark's proposed libel-courts seem to me about
as coercive as a newspaper's letter column (to get fair treatment on the
editorial page, you have to write).  They gain stature by reliably
publishing truth; while the participation of the accused helps, it is not
logically necessary to the process -- a libel-court that manages to give the
accused a fair trial in absentia will prosper.  There would be no point in
seeking a libel declaration from a forum without a reputation for fairness.
 
- - -
Mark Read Pickens said:
> Who designed this language, anyway?  It's a pain in the butt having
> multiple definitions for the same word!  :-(
 
But inevitable, unless either -- 
(a) the language-god infallibly foresees all the meanings anyone will ever
need and assigns a word to each; or
(b) whenever we need a meaning that has never been used before, we can coin
a word and be assured that it is unique and known to our peers.
 
The latter suggests a neural link to a universal lexicon-machine.  Amusing
concept.  Those who need a new word buy it from the machine; prices are
higher for short words (proportional to how many words of a given finite
length contain the word bought).  The owner's outlay is amortized, if that's
the term I want, by royalties if the meaning is in demand; if a word falls
into disuse, the machine can buy it back (and erase it from everyone's
memory).  The owner may at any time bid to trade a long word in on a
sportier model, to encourage its use and thereby attract more royalties.
 
An article in Scientific American pointed out that children learn something
like twenty thousand words in five or six thousand days; if this learning
can be duplicated artificially, the automatic lexicon should be easy.  ("The
Languages of Pao" meets cyberpunk?)
 
Once again, Extropian thought-space overlaps that appropriate to the
Constructed Languages list.  (conlang-request@buphy.bu.edu)
 
