Date: Fri, 1 Jan 1993 17:26:09 -0800
From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: SELF: alternate universes of the id?

Joseph Truitt said of his lucid dreams:
> Like David, I am puzzled by the fabricated memories in my dreams, but they
> do seem to make perfect sense IN the dream.  I wonder if there is any
> parallel between this phenomenon and a software program that runs one time
> with access to part of a database of rules and facts, and another time
> with a different, but overlapping, set of rules and facts.
 
I have had glimpses of those parallel memories in waking life -- I am
convinced for a moment that (say) Aunt Joyce never married, and the next
moment wonder how I could ever think that, when I have vivid mental images
of her second husband, and know that I have met her first husband and both
children (though their images have faded, I keep the catalog entry).
 
I suggest that this is a dysfunction or a (typically human) overgrowth of an
old (mammalian?) function of our brains.  We keep a running simulation of
the universe in our heads.  We constantly swap it with parallel images that
have elements added or removed, to see how these alternate universes behave.
 
It would make sense (I imagine) to keep, out of easy reach of the ego, a
storeroom of randomly-divergent universes, run them in "background" (idle
processing power) and learn what we can from them.
 
Perhaps the dream-generator goes to this storeroom for a universe to frame
the dream.  And sometimes an element from the storeroom leaks into
consciousness.
 
