Date:    Sat, 2 Jan 1993 1:03:22 -0800 (PST)
From: GRAPS@galileo.arc.nasa.gov
Subject: Selling Body Parts Hits Mainstream Science

There is a review of two books in the latest (Jan/Feb 1993) 
_The Sciences_ that I thought that the people on this list 
would be interested in. This is the first time that I can recall
the idea of selling body parts being taken seriously 
in a mainstream science magazine. I suppose this is a 
review of a review...

	"This Little Pancreas Went to Market" 
	by Tim Appelo

Books:

Sell Yourself to Science
(The Complete Guide to Selling Your Organs, Body
Fluids, Bodily Functions and Being a Human Guinea Pig)
by Jim Hogshire
Loompanics Unlimited, 160 pgs. $16.95

Spare Parts
(Organ Replacement in American Society)
by Renee C. Fox and Judith P. Swazey
Oxford University Press, 254 Pgs. $29.95

The reviewer presents a favorable attitude to the idea
of selling one's own organs. He starts by saying that selling
body parts  is a huge and growing business since the advent of the 
drug cyclosporine in the early 1980's, which enabled surgeons to 
delay the rejection response for months or years. But there is
a tremendous lack of available organs for organ recipients 
while at the same time, 80 percent of the healthiest, most
eminently recyclable cadavers are heedlessly discarded. Hogshire's
book points out that one's body is worth alot of money, but
because selling anything except blood, plasma, sperm, milk, 
(i.e. body fluids) is illegal, everyone *but* the donor is allowed 
to profit from organ transplants. Hogshire presents various
creative ways that one or one's family may still profit from
selling one's organs. 

Fox and Swazey's book is more dispassionate and discusses the 
prospects of legalizing the sale of organs and setting up a 
publicly traded futures market in Human Body Parts. They would
prefer to have insurance companies buy the rights to future harvest
of organs from the living. The book authors think that the distribution
of organs in this way will be more equitable than it is now.  The book
barely touches on some of the ethical questions such as: how far to go in 
keeping alive dead organ donors? or can a recipient who was to receive
an organ from a person who has taken up unhealthy habits sue that
person?

So get the magazine, and read this article! It's only about 5 pages
long, but  very interesting. It caught my eye right away by reviewing 
a book published  by Loompanics, my favorite wierd book catalog company. 
And THEN the article starts off talking about my favorite performing arts group:
Mark Pauline and Survival Research Labs. Two favorites in only the
first paragraph!

