Date: Sat, 2 Jan 1993 23:44:20 -0500
From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@ra.cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Re:  How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

     I think the problem of no visible intelligent activity in the known
Universe is extremely important, and would like to suggest a few little
thoughts of my own.
 
  The zoo hypotheses, an idea that we're being modelled and alike may
be good to know, but not fruitful.
 
  We can also suppose that the Universe/God has some law that speeds up
the civilizations' development at its late stages to pack all the
interesting and powerful parts into a limited time frame to make sure
different civilizations can take turns having fun in the limited
space / time.  But I would like to see the text of this law, and know
the implementation details...
 
  Also, we can see lots of entities with familiar features of consciousness
around us (e.g., philosophy in its studies of itself clearly demonstrates
developing reflective consciousness, etc.) that we choose not to consider
in our search for intelligence.
We want our aliens to be so similar to us that we define most of them out
of existence before starting the search.
Here I would suggest to look at some unconventional places.
E.g., vacuum fluctuations, internal structures of natural laws,
etc. might be much better habitats for intelligence than planetary surfaces.
 
  Still, there should be some civilizations that follow *our* path of
development.  Where are they?
Well, we wouldn't notice anything that hasn't surpassed us on this way,
because there isn't much to notice.
As for those far ahead, maybe we just don't understand where they - and we -
are going?  It might mean that the ideas of spatial and energetic expansion
get abandoned on early stages of civilization growth, and it takes entirely
new directions?  This I personally find very likely.
 
  We can also notice that whenever a breakthrough in development occurs,
the new stage is transparent for the old ones - they just can't see it
(at least, the emergent parts) - consider the line  rocks-animals-humans-
transhumans.
Also, the new stage's conventional parts become softer, gentler, and less
noticeable.
   Will dogs, if they decide to find us extropians, consider checking
Internet?  Or they will keep sniffing poles?
                
                        - - - - - 
 
  And, finally, I would suggest an explanation why the civilizations
like ours should collapse on early stages of their development.
An extropian filter, as Robin Hanson calls it.
 
  I still dearly keep an article from the Economist (!) explaining that
while closed systems tend to move to the state with highest entropy
and lowest potential energy, the open / growing systems develop to
a critical state (and stay there, balancing on the brink). This law
applies to a great variety of entities from sandpiles to computer
load to transportation systems.
 
   In real life, evolution is not a drift of the whole environment, it's
rather a stretch, with most of the parts staying where they are, and
the emergent ones moving on.
   Each new layer has two main tasks:  it finds new opportunities for
growth and comes up with certain regulatory mechanisms that direct and
contain the growth.  It may also regulate the previous layers, which
helps their growth and stability.
   The self-regulatory mechanisms might not be good enough to contain
growth, which may result in catastrophies of different scale.
 
   Fortunately, there hasn't been a single case in the development of
the Universe when a previous layer would attempt to regulate and control
the next, which would be a guarantee for disaster.
 
   Imagine a level of development whose representatives would proclaim
themselves the final stage of evolution and attempt to direct and control
any movement in the windows of opportunity opened by emerging groups,
without understanding - or wanting to understand - the directions of
development, fearing the future, but still eager to get some results
that *seem* within their reach?
 
    In my understanding, the 'Extropian Limit' for our civilization
may be represented by the line between conventional household human
types + the democratic and bureaucratic intitutions created to serve
them  and the new technological, social and conceptual growth represented
by a growing number of people and groups they can't understand but are
eager to regulate and milk.
 
    This line is being crossed right now.
 
  It may very well be the ultimate Limit to Growth (or life?) unless
the new entities shake the outdated regulation off and take this task
on themselves.  This isn't easy technically, and is additionally
complicated by the fact that it isn't politically correct (which means,
it doesn't correspond to the ethical criteria developed by the society
we want to break off from).
 
   Somebody called extropians "Evil geniuses for better tomorrow".
Looks like after a certain point geniuses should be evil - in a
conventional sense - to survive (and save everybody else).
                        
