interest, but it seems to me his analysis overlooks many nanotech
applications.  For example, he suggests an "SDI type defense" to protect
against meteorites.  Why bother?  Once we've been uploaded, assuming we have
sufficient redundancy, we simply repair the damage after impact--no harm
done.  

Uploading also addresses the issue of artificial gravity, scenery, even
population growth--if you don't have the resources to house a new
intelligence, where do you put it?  You don't.  Population growth stops
automatically.

Also, what's this about centuries of effort?  With relatively mature
nanotech, our species could construct Pokrovsky Shells effortlessly and
(historically speaking) in an eyeblink.

