   Can anyone explain to me why we should be able to see activity of
other civilizations? It is not obvious to me.  Right now, we can barely
detect planets only a few light years away, why should we be able to detect
the activity of civilizations hundreds of thousands of LY away?

   Are we basing this on the assumption that it is inevitable that an
advanced civilization will alter their star or build a dyson sphere or
ringworld? This doesn't seem at all obvious. A transcended civilization
may just conclude that they don't need such large habitats (because they
have uploaded). As for energy sources, there's no telling what the use.
If a dyson sphere existed, could we tell the difference between it and a 
black hole?

  I don't buy the von neumann machine argument either. If your sample size
is very large, then yes, sooner or later one civilization will try this
technique.  But what if there is a number, call it the anthropic number
(AN) which controls the number of civilizations that arise per universe.
If this number was just 2, or maybe 3, could we still expect to see
the other civilizations in the lifespan of our sun?

  I think the evidence is inconclusive as to whether other civilizations
exist.


