Date: Sun, 3 Jan 93 09:43:18 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject:  How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

Ethereal entities forced Alexander Chislenko <sasha@ra.cs.umb.edu> to write:
	I think the problem of no visible intelligent activity in the known
   Universe is extremely important, and would like to suggest a few little
   thoughts of my own.
   [...]
     Also, we can see lots of entities with familiar features of consciousness
   around us (e.g., philosophy in its studies of itself clearly demonstrates
   developing reflective consciousness, etc.) that we choose not to consider
   in our search for intelligence.

Sasha is proposing that philosophy itself, the science, is a conscious
entity.  I can't think of any useful definition of consciousness which
would make philosophy be alive, let alone conscious.  What are its
thoughts?  What would it be like to be "philosophy"?  If I were
philosophy, would there be just one of me?  What would the world look
like to me?  Could I move?  Could I act?

I hate to say it, but I think it's ridiculous to suggest that
philosophy is conscious.

   As for those far ahead, maybe we just don't understand where they - and we -
   are going?  It might mean that the ideas of spatial and energetic expansion
   get abandoned on early stages of civilization growth, and it takes entirely
   new directions?  This I personally find very likely.

As Perry and others have pointed out, the notion that a mature
civilization loses interest in such mundane goals as spatial expansion
has a big problem.  Although _most_ people in _most_ civilizations
might feel this way, if even a tiny percentage hold to the old
expansionistic ways, that percentage will still spread and numerically
overwhelm the others.  That's just evolution in action.  To use the
kind of rather strange analogy that I feel Sasha uses throughout this
post, maybe most of the bacteria were perfectly happy as they were,
but just a few decided to experiment with photosynthesis and make use
of that new abilty to expand.

     We can also notice that whenever a breakthrough in development occurs,
   the new stage is transparent for the old ones - they just can't see it
   (at least, the emergent parts) - consider the line  rocks-animals-humans-
   transhumans.

It's true that rocks can't see animals.  But they can't see rocks,
either.  That's because they can't see, period.  Animals can see
humans.  Humans should be able to see transhumans.  This is an example
of the inappropriate use of analogy.  It is simply not true that rocks
are to animals as animals are to humans.

      Will dogs, if they decide to find us extropians, consider checking
   Internet?  Or they will keep sniffing poles?

If dogs sniff legs, they can find us without needing Internet.
Similarly, if transhumans live in the physical universe, like people,
rocks, and animals, we can hope to perceive them.  The fact that they
would be engaging in many activities that we don't understand does not
change the point Robin made, which was that we should see the results
of what they do.  Dogs certainly see the results of human activity,
even if they don't understand them.

To make my point another way: we can imagine a human saying, I wonder
whether transhuman-like aliens exist in the universe.  What would be
the signs by which I could recognize them?  But we can't imagine an
animal saying the same thing about humans, because even to consider
the question implies human-level intelligence.  And we certainly can't
imagine a rock saying the same thing about animals, because rocks
don't even have any consciousness at all, hence they can't have any thoughts.

The moral is, transhumans will have many thoughts that we aren't
capable of.  But simple questions about existance are thoughts we are
capable of having, and there's no reason that trans-human-hood will
automatically lead to a state which transcends these questions, any
more than our own consciousness has removed us from the physical world
of rocks and animals.

      In real life, evolution is not a drift of the whole environment, it's
   rather a stretch, with most of the parts staying where they are, and
   the emergent ones moving on.
      Each new layer has two main tasks:  it finds new opportunities for
   growth and comes up with certain regulatory mechanisms that direct and
   contain the growth.  It may also regulate the previous layers, which
   helps their growth and stability.

Most of evolution occurs through small steps.  Maybe a bacterium
evolves a new enzyme which allows it to use a new food source.  This
matches Sasha's first paragraph above, where most bacteria might stay
in the old environment, but these emergent bacteria move into a new
niche.  This kind of change has happened countless times during
evolution.

But is this what Sasha has in mind as a new "layer"?  Does the new
bacteria regulate the previous bacteria, helping their growth and
stability?  I don't see that.  I would like to see some clarification
of what evolutionary changes constitute a "new layer".  Improved
teeth?  Better blood?  Wings?  These are the hard facts of evolution
and I'd like to see Sasha's speculations grounded in these.

      Imagine a level of development whose representatives would proclaim
   themselves the final stage of evolution and attempt to direct and control
   any movement in the windows of opportunity opened by emerging groups,
   without understanding - or wanting to understand - the directions of
   development, fearing the future, but still eager to get some results
   that *seem* within their reach?

Again, making the same criticism I have made before, this analysis can
only apply to conscious entitites.  Amoebas were not candidates for the
level of development described here - we can agree on this, right?
Sasha's political/sociological analysis may have some value, but it
would be clearer to express it in frankly political terms rather than
couching it in analogies about evolution, analogies which have little
value because of the vast differences between the changes we face
today and those changes which have occured through natural evolution.

